    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d271">

                <pb facs="1181" n="1119" sameAs="frus:frus1945v01/pg1119"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.CC/6–345: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-06-03T12:00:00+03:00">June 3,
                            1945—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received June 3—10:18 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1882. Before taking up the subject of your 1203, June 2, 6 p.m., it is vital that
                    we should fully understand the exact significance of Gromyko’s statement and its
                    relation to the agreement on voting procedure at Yalta.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Does his statement mean that the Soviet Govt is now demanding that there
                        must be an affirmative formal decision of the Council involving the full
                        right of veto by all five permanent members before any <pb facs="1182"
                            n="1120" xml:id="pg1120"/> situation can be brought up for discussion in
                        the Council, which in effect would vitiate the Yalta decision on voting
                        procedure since any situation or dispute would never reach that stage if one
                        of the permanent members wished to prevent it, or does Gromyko recognize
                        that if one of the permanent members is involved in the situation it would
                        abstain from voting in the initial decision?</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Am I right in my understanding that our position is that a free discussion
                        of a situation in the Council should be permitted prior to any formal vote
                        as to whether the Council should investigate such a situation?</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>Was there any formal or informal discussion of this aspect at Dumbarton
                        Oaks?</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>Can you fortify us with any arguments for use in our discussion here which
                        would indicate that Gromyko’s position is at variance with the intent of the
                        Yalta decision?</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
